# Task 11: Newton-Raphson and Secant Methods

## 1. Objective
The aim is to calculate the Internal Rate of Return (IRR) of an investment using the Newton-Raphson and Secant methods.

## 2. Inputs
The initial investment is 10,000. The annual cash inflows are 3,000, 3,500, 4,000 and 2,500.

## 3. Mathematical Operations
The program calculates the Net Present Value (NPV) and finds the interest rate at which NPV equals zero. Newton-Raphson uses the derivative of the NPV function. The Secant method estimates the slope using two starting values.

## 4. Original Output
Both methods calculate an IRR of approximately 11.542461%. The NPV at the calculated rate is approximately zero.

## 5. Modified Input
I changed the final cash inflow from 2,500 to 3,000.

## 6. Observation
Increasing the final cash inflow increases the calculated IRR from approximately 11.54% to 13.04%.

## 7. Interpretation
A higher IRR indicates a higher rate of return based on the projected cash flows. However, IRR alone does not determine whether an investment is suitable.

## 8. Related Real-World Problem
The program can help compare investment projects by calculating the expected rate of return from their initial costs and future cash inflows.

## 9. Assumptions
The initial investment occurs at year zero. The cash inflows occur annually, and the projected amounts are assumed to be accurate.

## 10. New Input Data
The final annual cash inflow was increased from 2,500 to 3,000, while the other cash flows remained unchanged.

## 11. Modified Output
Both methods should calculate an IRR of approximately 13.038165%.

## 12. Interpretation of the Modified Problem
The increased final cash inflow makes the investment more profitable according to the IRR calculation. Both methods should still produce approximately the same result.